# Before writing any code

Read `ARCHITECTURE.md` and `PROGRESS.md` in the repo root. They are load-bearing,
not background reading: `ARCHITECTURE.md` states the five non-negotiable rules
below and the module map; `PROGRESS.md` is the phase-by-phase log of what was
built, what was measured, and every judgment call and gap left behind. Update
both when a phase of work is done.

## The five rules (full detail in `ARCHITECTURE.md`)

1. **Determinism.** Every piece of world content is a pure function of
   `(worldSeed, coordinate)`. Use the counter-based hashes in `src/core/hash.ts`,
   never a sequential PRNG. No `Math.pow`, `sin`, `cos`, or `exp` on any path that
   ends in a stored vertex — ECMAScript only approximates those.
2. **No global mutable world state.** Chunks unload and regenerate at any time
   and must come back byte-identical.
3. **Tiered generation.** `Region (4km) -> Sector (512m) -> Chunk (64m)`. A finer
   tier may read a coarser one; a coarser tier may **never** read a finer one.
4. **Stable interfaces.** `src/world/contracts.ts` is load-bearing. Adding a
   field to `ChunkData` is normal; reshaping `ChunkCoord`, `ChunkProvider`, or
   `TierContext` requires stopping to explain the change before writing code.
5. **Hard budgets**, checked every phase: 60fps @ 1080p, <=1200 draw calls,
   <=400MB heap after 5 min, <=16ms frame / no >4ms GC spike. Re-derive with a
   measured number when a phase moves them; never raise a limit quietly.

## Layering (`src/world/`)

Only `chunk-mesh.ts` imports Three.js. Everything else — `contracts.ts`,
`noise.ts`, `height-field.ts`, `rivers.ts`, `roads.ts`, `streets.ts`,
`grading.ts`, `chunk-gen.ts` — is pure and runs identically in a worker and in
a Node test. Keep it that way: the moment generation code imports Three, it
stops being testable without a browser.

## Verification, before calling anything done

```
npm test               # vitest; must pass
npx tsc --noEmit        # must be clean
npm run shots:check    # byte-identical screenshots; only if rendering changed
npm run soak           # 5-minute flight; heap trend, budgets, anti-vacuity guards
```

Every check in this project pairs a positive assertion with an anti-vacuity
guard (e.g. "roads exist" AND "the flight actually passed one"). When adding a
feature, add both halves — a check that only ever proves absence passes
perfectly on a world with none of the feature in it.

## Update `PROGRESS.md` before calling a phase done

This is not optional, and it is not a summary written from memory afterward —
write it as you go, the way every existing phase entry reads. A phase is not
finished until `PROGRESS.md` has a section covering:

- **What was built and why**, plus every judgment call worth knowing (the
  entries in this project favour "here is the alternative we rejected and why"
  over "here is what we did").
- **What was measured**, with real numbers: the soak's budget table (draw
  calls, triangles, vertices, payload bytes, heap trend) re-derived and
  compared against the previous phase's row, not just "unchanged" asserted.
- **Known gaps, deliberately left**, so the next phase (or the next session)
  does not have to rediscover them by reading code.
- **A "for next phase" note** naming anything the next piece of work will need
  to know that isn't obvious from the code alone.

If `ARCHITECTURE.md` itself changed shape — a new module, a changed rule, a new
non-negotiable boundary — update it too, in the same commit as the code.
